# CHAPTER 15 — THREE YEARS: WHAT HAS BEEN BUILT?

## The Waypoint

At three years old, a child is already recognizably a person.

Not finished. Not fully formed. Not independent.

But no longer simply an infant.

The child can move through the world with increasing purpose, communicate with words and gestures, remember what happened yesterday, anticipate what may happen next, pretend that one object is another, seek comfort from a familiar person, protest an unwanted instruction, imitate what other people do, and begin to organize behavior around rules and goals.

Something substantial has been built.

Yet the most important conclusion of developmental science is that what has been built by three is not a completed human being. It is a foundation: biologically substantial, psychologically meaningful, socially organized, and still open to reorganization.

Age three is therefore best understood as a waypoint.

The distinction matters because two opposite mistakes have repeatedly shaped ideas about early childhood. One treats the first years as destiny: whatever happens early is assumed to become permanently encoded in personality, intelligence, or emotional life. The other treats early development as largely provisional: whatever happens before formal schooling is dismissed as something that later education will simply overwrite.

Neither description fits the evidence.

Early development matters.

It also changes.

The first three years involve extraordinary developmental transformation across biological, cognitive, emotional, and social systems. At the same time, continuity across development is generally moderate rather than absolute, and resilience, environmental change, and intervention can alter trajectories. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

The question at three is therefore not:

> What has this child become forever?

It is:

> What has been built so far, what remains fragile, and what possibilities remain open?

