## I. What Has Been Built?

Development does not produce a collection of isolated abilities.

It produces coordination.

A newborn begins life with capacities for sensing, orienting, learning, moving, and responding, but these systems operate with limited integration. Across the first three years, they become increasingly coordinated.

The body becomes more regulated.

Attention becomes more selective.

Memory becomes more useful.

Action becomes more intentional.

Communication becomes symbolic.

Emotion becomes increasingly organized.

Relationships become differentiated.

Behavior becomes more socially intelligible.

Play becomes representational.

And the child becomes increasingly able to act on the world while also constructing an internal model of it.

The development of these systems is not linear. Each new capacity modifies the conditions under which the others can develop.

Language, for example, is not simply another skill added to cognition. It becomes a tool for organizing attention, representing goals, communicating needs, remembering rules, and participating in shared meaning. Earlier chapters showed how language grows from prelinguistic communication, perceptual learning, social attention, memory, and interaction rather than appearing suddenly with the first spoken word. The evidence likewise warns against treating vocabulary size as a complete measure of linguistic experience or development. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3} :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

Play reveals the same architecture from another direction.

By the third year, many children can use objects, actions, roles, and narratives to represent situations beyond the immediate present. But pretend play is not a self-contained engine of intelligence. Its meaning depends upon developing language, representation, executive skills, motivation, social participation, materials, and context. Play is an arena in which these capacities can be exercised and coordinated; it is not a magical ingredient that guarantees later success. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

The developmental achievement, then, is not the possession of isolated abilities.

It is increasing coordination among them.

