## II. The Biological Foundation

The first three years are a period of extraordinary biological change.

Neural systems expand, reorganize, establish new patterns of connectivity, and become increasingly specialized. Sensory and associative systems develop rapidly, while systems supporting higher-order control remain immature and continue developing for many years. The resulting brain is neither unfinished in the sense of being incapable nor finished in the sense of being fixed. It is highly organized and highly plastic at the same time. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

The same principle applies to physiological regulation.

During infancy, regulation is heavily dependent on relationships and context. By three, sleep-wake organization, autonomic regulation, attention, and behavioral control have become more structured, but they remain sensitive to stress, fatigue, novelty, and environmental support.

The developing child therefore reaches the third birthday carrying a biological history.

Some of that history reflects prenatal conditions.

Some reflects nutrition, illness, and physical environment.

Some reflects stress and protection.

Some reflects repeated patterns of interaction.

And some reflects characteristics of the child that are not reducible to any single environmental cause.

This is why developmental biology cannot be translated into simple programming.

Early experience can influence biological systems. It does not write an irreversible script.

The appropriate metaphor is not a machine being assembled according to fixed instructions.

It is a system being organized through repeated interaction.

