## III. From Regulation to Self-Organization

The infant begins life with limited capacity to manage arousal independently.

The caregiver helps.

This does not mean that the infant is passive. The child signals distress, turns toward or away from stimulation, explores, seeks proximity, and modifies the behavior of the adult. Regulation is transactional from the beginning.

Across the first three years, the balance gradually changes.

The child becomes increasingly able to redirect attention, delay an action briefly, use familiar routines, communicate emotional states, seek help deliberately, and recover from some forms of distress without immediate physical intervention.

But the change is not from dependence to independence.

It is from predominantly external regulation toward increasingly distributed regulation.

The child can do more alone because more capacities now exist within the child.

Yet those capacities remain embedded in relationships.

The three-year-old who can calm down after frustration may still need a familiar adult when exhausted.

The child who can remember a rule may still break it when overwhelmed.

The child who can manage a simple frustration may lose that ability when hungry, frightened, overstimulated, or confronted with an unfamiliar demand.

Developmental competence is therefore state-dependent.

This point is essential because behavior at three can otherwise be mistaken for character.

A child who fails a self-control task is not necessarily a child who "lacks discipline."

A child who has a tantrum is not necessarily oppositional.

A child who becomes overwhelmed by novelty is not necessarily incapable.

The developing system has limits.

The task of development is to gradually enlarge the circumstances in which a child can use the capacities they already possess.

