## IV. The Child Becomes a Communicating Person

Perhaps the clearest transformation of the first three years is the movement from signaling to shared symbolic meaning.

Before words, the infant already participates in communication through gaze, vocalization, facial expression, movement, rhythm, and turn-taking.

Later, gestures and shared attention make it possible to coordinate attention around something outside the dyad.

Then words begin to connect sound with objects, people, actions, intentions, and events.

Grammar begins to organize relations among those words.

Language becomes increasingly useful not only for naming the world, but for representing it.

This development changes the child's relationship with experience.

A person can now be referred to when absent.

A remembered event can be discussed.

A future event can be anticipated.

A desire can be negotiated.

A rule can be represented.

A feeling can be named.

A possibility can be imagined.

The child becomes increasingly capable of living not only in the immediate environment, but in a shared symbolic world.

Language is therefore both an outcome of development and one of its organizing forces.

Yet even here, caution is necessary.

The development of language varies widely across children and cultures. Receptive and expressive language should not be collapsed into one measure. Word counts alone do not capture the richness of linguistic experience. Conversational participation, shared attention, social context, and the opportunities available to the child all matter. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

By three, language has become one of the child's principal tools for thinking with other people.

It has not, however, finished developing.

