## V. From One Relationship to a Social World

Early development begins in intimate relationships, but it does not remain there.

Across the first three years, the child's social world expands.

A parent or primary caregiver is joined by other caregivers, siblings, grandparents, childcare providers, peers, and increasingly the wider social environment.

The developmental unit is therefore not simply a child and one adult.

It is a network.

The significance of that network lies not in its size but in what relationships make possible: regulation, protection, communication, learning, play, cultural participation, and continuity.

The research reviewed for the book supports the existence of multiple meaningful caregiver relationships but does not support the simpler claim that more caregivers are always better. Relationship quality, continuity, coordination, material conditions, culture, and the characteristics of the child all matter. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}

By three, the child is therefore no longer developing inside a single relational environment.

They are participating in an ecology.

That ecology can either reinforce or complicate the capacities that are emerging.

A responsive home can be undermined by severe instability elsewhere.

A supportive childcare setting can expand developmental opportunities.

Economic stress can reduce the time and psychological bandwidth available for responsive interaction.

Stable relationships can buffer adversity.

Institutional deprivation can disrupt development dramatically.

This is why parenting cannot be separated from the conditions that enable parenting.

The child develops in relationships.

Those relationships develop inside systems.

