## VI. Agency Emerges

Another major change by age three is the emergence of recognizable agency.

The infant learns that actions can produce effects.

The toddler discovers that objects behave differently depending on what they do with them.

The child experiments.

They drop things.

They repeat actions.

They imitate.

They test limits.

They discover that some outcomes can be predicted and others cannot.

This is the logic of the developing mind in motion: attention selects experience, action changes the information available, outcomes generate new evidence, and unexpected results can trigger updating. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}

Agency is therefore not simply a personality characteristic.

It is a developmental achievement.

The child progressively becomes capable of doing things on purpose, understanding consequences, selecting among alternatives, and using symbols to guide action.

Behavioral development can consequently be understood not as the suppression of impulse but as the social organization of agency.

Boundaries matter because action takes place in a world shared with other people.

Rules matter because other people's needs and intentions matter.

The developing child is learning not merely how to obey, but how to act within a social reality.

That is a much more significant achievement.

