## VII. What Has Not Yet Been Built?

For all that has emerged by three, much remains immature.

Executive control is still fragile.

Working memory is limited.

Cognitive flexibility remains inconsistent.

Emotional regulation depends heavily on context.

Peer conflict still overwhelms many children.

Abstract reasoning is rudimentary.

Self-control can collapse under strong arousal.

Perspective-taking is emerging rather than complete.

The child can represent possibilities but still confuses imagination and reality in important situations.

Three years of development have created a functioning architecture.

They have not created an adult one.

This distinction matters because developmental milestones are often interpreted as if they mark completed systems.

They do not.

A milestone generally means that a capability has become sufficiently available to appear under some conditions.

It does not mean the child can deploy it reliably in every environment.

The three-year-old may know the rule and still fail to follow it.

They may understand another person's feelings and still behave selfishly.

They may know a word and not retrieve it when frustrated.

They may be capable of waiting for thirty seconds and unable to wait for five minutes.

Development is not the disappearance of inconsistency.

It is the gradual expansion of competence across conditions.

---

## VIII. What Persists?

The evidence does not support the idea that everything at three is temporary.

Some early differences show continuity.

Temperamental characteristics can remain recognizable over time.

Early cognitive and language differences can carry predictive information into later childhood.

Behavioral patterns can forecast elevated population-level risks.

Early self-control is associated with later outcomes across multiple domains.

These findings matter.

If nothing persisted, early development would have little long-term significance.

But continuity should not be mistaken for identity.

Longitudinal research generally shows moderate rather than perfect stability, and stability tends to increase with age. The same child can change substantially in relative standing as developmental demands, social environments, and individual capacities change. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}

This creates an important distinction:

**A tendency can persist without becoming destiny.**

A shy child may become more socially confident.

A highly reactive child may become increasingly regulated.

A child with early cognitive difficulties may catch up.

A child who appears advanced early may cease to stand out.

Development contains both continuity and reorganization.

The scientifically honest answer is not either/or.

It is both.

---

## IX. What Fades?

One of the most revealing findings in intervention science is that some early differences do not persist in their original form.

Children who receive an enriched educational experience may show substantial early gains on standardized cognitive measures. Those differences can diminish over time.

This phenomenon is known as fadeout.

But fadeout does not necessarily mean that nothing happened.

An early intervention may change behavior, educational participation, relationships, confidence, or the probability of encountering particular institutional pathways even after an initial test-score advantage becomes smaller.

The evidence reviewed for this project therefore treats persistence as domain-specific. Some gains weaken. Some persist. Some reappear later in different forms. Others alter the pathway rather than the endpoint. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}

This is one reason why developmental outcomes cannot be reduced to a single score measured at a single age.

A childhood intervention can change a trajectory without permanently separating two groups on the same test.

Likewise, an early disadvantage can diminish without having been irrelevant.

Development is historical.

What happens at one point changes the conditions for what can happen later.

