## X. Recovery Is Part of Development

The existence of vulnerability does not imply irreversibility.

Some of the strongest evidence for developmental plasticity comes from children who experienced severe early deprivation and were later moved into more supportive caregiving environments.

The results are neither sentimental nor fatalistic.

Early deprivation can produce substantial harm.

But improved environments can also produce substantial recovery.

The Bucharest Early Intervention Project and related longitudinal evidence demonstrate both sides of this equation: severe deprivation matters, timing matters, and recovery remains possible. Not every child fully recovers, and not every domain responds equally. But the existence of substantial catch-up directly contradicts the idea that early adversity simply writes a permanent developmental sentence. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}

This leads to one of the book's most important distinctions:

**Plasticity is not the opposite of vulnerability.**

A system can be highly plastic precisely because it is highly sensitive.

The same openness that allows experience to shape development also creates opportunities for correction when environments improve.

That is why intervention remains worthwhile long after the earliest developmental window has passed.

