## XI. Resilience Is Not Invulnerability

The word resilience can easily become another form of mythology.

It can imply that some children possess a special internal toughness that protects them from adversity.

The evidence points elsewhere.

Resilience commonly arises from ordinary adaptive systems.

A dependable relationship.

A functioning cognitive system.

Growing self-regulation.

Access to supportive institutions.

A community that provides continuity.

Opportunities to participate and learn.

These are not extraordinary protections.

They are ordinary developmental supports.

Masten's concept of "ordinary magic" captures this principle: resilience is often the normal operation of basic adaptive systems rather than evidence of a rare psychological trait. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}

This matters for both families and societies.

A resilient child should not be interpreted as proof that adversity was harmless.

A child who struggles should not be interpreted as lacking resilience.

Resilience is a process.

It depends upon what the environment makes possible.

